{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527137099,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE2NDMzMjkzNzA="},"added":["windows"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527154299,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTY1MTAyOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391651029"},"message":"FTR: At a first glance, I suspect the colon in `C:\\` to cause a parsing issue here ...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527172859,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTczOTgyNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391739826"},"message":"Ah, no. The input is `ssh://mars:Zoidberg` not `c:\\` in it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527173873,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTc0NTk1Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391745952"},"message":"Sure. But I recall sth like first trying to interpret it as a path, which then would lead to assuming a relative path (since it's obviously not absolute), therefore trying to convert to sth like `C:\\somewhere\\ssh://mars:Zoidberg`. Something along these lines ... Of course my remembrance might be entirely wrong or simply outdated.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527174407,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTc0OTI0Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391749246"},"message":"I'm afraid I'm pretty close with the guess above. `clone` does sth very similar and is NOT using `RI` capabilities first.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527175595,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTc1NjM5Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391756396"},"message":"Actually, I start wondering why the hell it raises on linux ;-)\nAnyways - I'm on it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527176504,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTc2MjAyNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391762027"},"message":"Indeed: Even on linux, `clone` concludes the target to be sth like `/tmp/datalad-test/ssh:/mars:Zoidberg`.\nWhat's causing the expected Exception is in fact the `Dataset` constructor, not some `RI` stuff. It should fail before and use `RI` to make sure, `path` is in fact a `PathRI`. I'll fix that.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527234001,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MTk2OTQ5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2545#issuecomment-391969492"},"message":"Any progress? After enabling SSH tests on windows the issue reveals itself as rather wide-spread:\n\nhttps://ci.appveyor.com/project/mih/datalad-5sgef/build/1.0.91","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1527249701,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MjAzMTg1MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-392031851"},"message":"Yes, except it's supposed to return a (part of) URL, which might confusingly be called `path`. In that case it should still return a \"POSIX path\" but it needs to be called `url` instead. Just saying, since the implicit assumption that a path can be treated that way is still widespread.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1527256412,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MjA2NDkzMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-392064930"},"message":"yes, making it clear(er) where a \"path\" component is the \"urlpath\" (or \"posixpath\") would be useful!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527483887,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MjQxOTk4MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-392419981"},"message":"So that is settled.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1527483887,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTY0ODYzNTQxMg=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528557038,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5NTk3NjY5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-395976690"},"message":"I am reopening this: https://bytes.com/topic/python/answers/19793-convert-between-windows-style-paths-posix-style-paths\n\nIt looks like we are doing it wrong, and NT supports POSIX paths, just not at the command prompt. Hence we might want to rethink our judgement.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528557038,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEzOlJlb3BlbmVkRXZlbnQxNjcyMjkzMDUy"},"status":1},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1531855557,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwNTY5OTI3MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-405699271"},"message":"just 1c in favor of (2) -- users will specify native to the system paths and ideally they should get paths reported to them in the same fashion, otherwise confusions such as https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2643#issuecomment-405036688 might keep happening where the reason for incorrect behavior does not become immediately apparent","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1531856198,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwNTcwMjI2Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-405702267"},"message":"From the aforementioned link about support of `/` in NT paths (which I've verified briefly to indeed work with basic Python's `open` call) it is not yet clear how \"full path\" would look like since it must contain the drive letter, and I think it is the MSYS*/MINGW* internal convention on mapping it to `/X/` which also somewhat fails since if there is any single lettered directory (not even conflicting) in the directory they consider `/` it is not made available within this `/` pathname.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1531856329,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwNTcwMjgzMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-405702832"},"message":"There is not really THE full path. There are DOS paths (with drive letter) and UNC paths, boths are supported by NT","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1531856943,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwNTcwNTY1NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-405705654"},"message":"heh heh, ok -- call it \"absolute\" path not a \"full\" one.\nBoth conventions (DOS and UNC) are not posix, include server or drive within, and rely on back slashes.  So what am I missing on how they could/should be mapped into POSIX paths for internal manipulations if we were to go \"all posix\" way? (e.g. \"use UNC and replace all backslashes with forward slashes, hip hip hoorra!\")","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1531857308,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQwNTcwNzM0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-405707344"},"message":"I think step one is for us to not worry about backslashes at all, as NT handles slashes just fine in both variants of absolute paths. That makes any relative path POSIX compatible. This leaves absolute paths. For those I would be leaning to UNC, but I gind it hard to predict the type and amount of fallout.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538322096,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTcyOTc5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-425729795"},"message":"I have now verified that at least from Windows Vista onwards, POSIX paths typed/copied into the windows terminal as arguments of datalad commands yield the desired behavior.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538574799,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjY0NTgwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-426645806"},"message":"I spend the day wrangling paths, and I think the core problem would be elegantly solved by using Path instances from `pathlib` (https://docs.python.org/3/library/pathlib.html). A backport for PY2 is available.\n\nI am implementing a demo in https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/1 now.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1538576386,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjY1NTIxNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-426655216"},"message":"So the idea is to have pathlib's Paths which would be returned/stored (`.path`) throughout the API?\n\nnote, backported for python2 package is called pathlib2 (python-pathlib2 in debian)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538594590,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNjc2NjEwMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-426766103"},"message":"In the PR linked above you can see how this would work (incl. deps). I went for `.pathobj` to be incremental.\n\nMore importantly, we should switch from abspaths in result dicts to `Path` object to harvest the benefits... eventually.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540755298,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzczNDYxMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-433734610"},"message":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution now uses pathlib for internal handling and sticks with path string in the result records to minimize impact on datalad-core.\n\nPath resolution works a bit different (is guaranteed to become abspath underneath the reference dataset, no matter how originally specified). Path handover dataset\u003c-\u003e is happening in high-level API code (not a low-level decorator) to be able to see the full context, including any descrepancy between dataset and repo paths.\n\nThis seems to work quite well on all platforms (incl. Windows). I will close this for now until real-world exposure tells me how for this holds up.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1540755298,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTkzMDkyODczNQ=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1540766096,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMzc0ODI3MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2546#issuecomment-433748271"},"message":"What about adding a label fixed-in-revolution and keep it open here since issue remains in this code base","files":null}]}